{"id":"b6b6727d-172d-44b3-979f-c7d69abc4470","name":"Passing Dynamic Resource IDs for Coins","timestamp":1742497373857,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"<user_query>
i need to pass for each Coin a R.id.(symbol string). 

how can i do this
</user_query>"},{"role":"assistant","content":"I understand you want to dynamically convert the coin's symbol to a corresponding drawable resource ID. Here's how you can modify the code to achieve this:

```kotlin:app/src/main/java/com/plcoding/cryptotracker/crypto/presentation/models/CoinUi.kt
fun Coin.toCoinUi(): CoinUi {
 return CoinUi(
 // ... existing code ...
 iconRes = try {
 val resourceField = R.drawable::class.java.getField(symbol.lowercase())
 resourceField.getInt(null)
 } catch (e: Exception) {
 // Provide a default icon if the resource is not found
 R.drawable.default_coin // You'll need to create this default drawable
 }
 )
}
```

Here's what this does:

1. It attempts to get the drawable resource ID dynamically by accessing the R.drawable class using reflection
2. The symbol is converted to lowercase since resource names in Android can't contain uppercase letters
3. If the resource isn't found, it falls back to a default icon

A few important notes:
- You'll need to ensure that your drawable resources are named exactly like the coin symbols but in lowercase (e.g., "btc.xml", "eth.xml", etc.)
- Make sure to create a default_coin drawable resource to handle cases where a matching icon isn't found
- Resource names in Android can only contain lowercase letters, numbers, and underscores

Alternative approach without reflection (safer but more verbose):
```kotlin:app/src/main/java/com/plcoding/cryptotracker/crypto/presentation/models/CoinUi.kt
fun getDrawableForSymbol(symbol: String): Int {
 return when (symbol.lowercase()) {
 "btc" -> R.drawable.btc
 "eth" -> R.drawable.eth
 // Add other symbols as needed
 else -> R.drawable.default_coin
 }
}

fun Coin.toCoinUi(): CoinUi {
 return CoinUi(
 // ... existing code ...
 iconRes = getDrawableForSymbol(symbol)
 )
}
```

The second approach is safer but requires manual maintenance of the mapping. Choose the approach that better fits your needs considering the trade-offs between maintenance and flexibility."}]}
